 A browser frame of the REAL route, SCRUBBED -- every value on the page a function
 of one number `u` running 0..1.

 THIS CHAPTER USED TO BE THE DRIVEN PAGE AS WELL, and it is not any more. The
 driven half -- the state fold, the live camera, the frame's own sequence and the
 dispatch over the depth sort -- is `Safari chapter Safari`, a port of the game's
 own safari.zig, and this is a poc again: a still at a chosen point on the route,
 which is what it was written to be. What is left here is the scrub itself.

 This replaces poc/Scene, which placed its own scenery by hand because almost
 nothing was ported when it was written. Nothing here is placed by hand. The road,
 its corners, the conifers, the intersection towers, the guard rails and the pond
 all come out of `Safari chapter World` -- the same nineteen segments graded at
 3,822 values -- mapped by Render's chain and ordered by Render's depth sort.

 IT IS STILL A POC, AND HERE IS EXACTLY WHAT IS NOT REAL ABOUT IT.

 THE ANIMALS ARE HERE NOW. Their art is `Safari chapter EmojiStills`, generated
 from the game's own baked table by harness/bake_stills.py rather than
 hand-carried, and `Safari chapter Critter` places it. One still per species, 129
 polygons in all -- nothing per-frame is baked; the animals are static billboards
 that scale with distance, and every bit of motion in this scene is computed.

 THE BULL DRAWS FLAT. It is the only Fluent Color animal and 40 of its 43 polygons
 carry a 2-stop gradient, which paint.zig writes as wire tags 5 and 6 that Paint
 does not model yet, so the generated table flattens each to its first stop.

 THE CAT DRAWS TOO, from `Safari chapter CatStills` -- seven stills, not seven
 hundred, with `Safari chapter Cat` picking one per crossing step and computing the
 lateral travel and the hop continuously.

 AND THE TRUCK IS HERE, drawn by `Safari chapter TruckBody`.

 THE RIDER IS NOT SIMULATED ON THIS PAGE, and that is what makes it a scrub rather
 than a drive. `frame-at u` has to answer from u alone, so u walks the route by arc
 length at a constant speed, dead centre of the lane, looking straight down it: no
 lean, no gaze, no throttle, and a truck riding its schedule rather than its three
 rules. The driven page has all of those and is `Safari chapter Safari`.

 The backdrop heading is BLENDED ACROSS EACH CORNER rather than taken from the
 rider. A segment's north-heading is a step function -- it jumps by the whole turn
 at the joint -- and the mountains are drawn from it, so taken raw the entire
 horizon would snap sideways as the rider crosses. The real game does not have this
 problem because rider.zig turns him continuously. Blending over the last stretch
 of each segment is a stand-in for that, and it is the one number on this page that
 is invented rather than ported.

 We say:

Section: The Scrub

 u runs 0..1 and the shim advances it. Everything on the page is a function of it.

 The sunset clock for a SCRUBBED still, where there is no frame history to count.
 The driven page keeps a real frame counter (see The Real Rider below); this maps
 the scrub onto the same range by taking the route to be about 6,400 frames, which
 is what it measures at the rider's own speeds. Near enough for a still, and the
 stills are the only thing that still uses it.

  route-frames : Real
  route-frames = 6400.0

  scene-step-at : Real -> Real
  scene-step-at (u) = route-frames * u

 HOW FAR u MOVES IN ONE FRAME, AND IT IS THE ROUTE THAT DECIDES, not a constant
 picked to look right. rider.zig measures speed in METRES PER FRAME -- v-base 0.3,
 v-max 2.5 -- so a frame covers about two metres once he is up to speed and
 braking for corners. u is arc length over the whole course, so one frame is that
 distance divided by the course.

 The scrub had a flat 1/900, which over a 7.5 km course is 8.4 m a frame: more
 than three times the rider's top speed, and it looked it. Deriving it from
 v-max means the page cannot drift away from the game's own pace again, and a
 route change moves it automatically.

  drive-speed : Real
  drive-speed = v-max * 0.8

  u-per-step : Real
  u-per-step = drive-speed / (course-length build-world * 0.995)

Section: Where On The Route

 THE WORLD IS THREADED, NOT REFERENCED, and that is a performance fix rather than
 a style choice. A nullary Codex binding emits as a zig FUNCTION, not a memoised
 constant -- `segs = build-world` became `fn segs() { return build_world(); }` --
 so every mention rebuilt all nineteen segments with their trees, herds and cat
 placements. `pos-from` mentions it twice per loop iteration, so one frame rebuilt
 the world hundreds of times: 270 ms a frame, about four per second. Passing it
 down as a parameter, the way every graded chapter in the port already does,
 builds it once. See PORTING_NOTES B13.

 How far along the whole course, in metres. Stopping just short of the end keeps
 the walk below inside the last segment rather than falling off it.

  drive-dist-at : List Segment, Real -> Real
  drive-dist-at (w) (u) = course-length w * u * 0.995

  RoutePos = record {
    seg : Integer,
    along : Real
  }

  pos-from : List Segment, Real, Integer -> RoutePos
  pos-from (w) (dist) (i) =
    if i + 1 >= list-length w then RoutePos { seg = i, along = dist }
    else if dist < (list-at w i).length then RoutePos { seg = i, along = dist }
    else pos-from w (dist - (list-at w i).length) (i + 1)

  drive-pos-at : List Segment, Real -> RoutePos
  drive-pos-at (w) (u) = pos-from w (drive-dist-at w u) 0

 THE BLENDED HEADING, and the reason it exists is in this chapter's opening. A
 segment's north-heading jumps by the whole exit turn at the joint; blending into
 the next one over the last stretch keeps the mountains from snapping sideways.

  turn-blend-dist : Real
  turn-blend-dist = 120.0

  heading-of : List Segment, RoutePos -> Real
  heading-of (w) (p) =
    let sg = list-at w p.seg
    in if p.seg + 1 >= list-length w then sg.north-heading
      else blend-heading sg (list-at w (p.seg + 1)) p.along

  blend-heading : Segment, Segment, Real -> Real
  blend-heading (sg) (nx) (along) =
    let left = sg.length - along
    in if left >= turn-blend-dist then sg.north-heading
      else sg.north-heading + (nx.north-heading - sg.north-heading) * (turn-blend-dist - left) / turn-blend-dist

  heading-in : List Segment, Real -> Real
  heading-in (w) (u) = heading-of w (drive-pos-at w u)

  scene-heading-at : Real -> Real
  scene-heading-at (u) = heading-in build-world u

 The shim reads the sun straight off Sky, as the old scene did.

  scene-sun-at : Real -> SunPos
  scene-sun-at (u) = sun-pos (heading-in build-world u) (scene-step-at u) focal camera-w

Section: The Pose

 Dead centre of the lane, looking straight down it. across and yaw are the two
 numbers the rider would own, and both are zero here.

  pose-in : List Segment, Real -> Pose
  pose-in (w) (u) =
    let p = drive-pos-at w u
    in Pose { along = p.along, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, hw = (list-at w p.seg).width / 2.0 }

Section: The Frame

 Backdrop, then the floor, then everything that stands up in depth order. That is
 render.zig's own sequence.

  frame-at : Real -> List DrawCmd
  frame-at (u) = frame-in build-world u

 THE SCRUBBED TRUCK RIDES ITS SCHEDULE. There is no state to step here -- a still
 has to answer from u alone -- so it is placed where the chase says it should be
 for this rider distance, which is `truck-schedule` and not a number invented on
 this page. It never brakes, because braking is a comparison against a previous
 speed and a still has no previous. Same shape as the scrubbed rider above, and
 the driven page below does not use any of it.

  frame-in : List Segment, Real -> List DrawCmd
  frame-in (w) (u) =
    let p = drive-pos-at w u
    in let pose = pose-in w u
    in let step = scene-step-at u
    in let ch = build-chain w p.seg
    in let tk = truck-schedule (drive-dist-at w u) (course-length w)
    in (draw (heading-in w u) (sun-set-fraction step) focal camera-w)
       & (frame-ground w p.seg pose focal camera-w)
       & (draw-order w ch pose (collect w p.seg pose focal p.along 0.0 tk) False focal step 0)

  frame : List DrawCmd
  frame = frame-at 0.0
